 "note": [
  "Each chapter’s KJV epigraph is replaced by the whole verse verbatim from Louis Segond (1910), with only French typographic spacing added before : and ; and the “(KJV)” label dropped, as in a-retrospect-teens.fr. Where Segond’s verse ends on a comma or semicolon (Ephesians 4:26, 1 Corinthians 1:27) the epigraph closes with a full stop instead; Psalm 90:12 has the same number in Segond.",
  "Athanasius’s and Antony’s quoted sentences reuse the wording of the shipped French full edition (life-of-antony.fr, « Vie d’Antoine ») wherever the teen edition quotes him: the bee, the tomb cry, « Où étais-tu ? », the silver dish and the gold, the « ni gras » description, the talk to the monks, the daily counsel, Maximin and the governor, the philosophers, the emperors, the last words and the sheepskin. Paul’s « Je meurs chaque jour » (1 Corinthians 15:31) follows that edition’s wording, not Segond’s « Chaque jour je suis exposé à la mort », so it is self_rendered.",
  "Names follow French usage: « Antoine » (the English never says “Saint Antony”, so no « saint »), Athanase, Alexandrie, le Nil, Maximin, Constantin; “cloak” is « manteau » and the sheepskin « peau de brebis » (the full edition’s term). The 300 acres are kept as « trois cents arpents » (the full edition’s figure) with « environ cent vingt hectares » added. Title « Vie d’Antoine (Pour ados) » is 25 characters.",
  "Narration is in the passé simple and the reader is addressed with « tu », as in a-retrospect-teens.fr; the weekly prompt is « Une chose à faire cette semaine : », dropping the English’s repeated “this week” inside the sentence.",
  "AI-translated in-session; awaiting native review."
 ],
